{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2663 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "15a52aafe9a31510b2979fd3afe2ffb26b3830b4e58f89d8fe42faec41c6485a" :edb/db-before-t 2662 :edb/db-after-t 2663 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535975 50 #inst "2026-10-15T14:13:12.889Z" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052450 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052451 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052452 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052453 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186052454 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550437 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550456 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550538 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550885 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550946 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186052449 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550538 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054668 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551249 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054669 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551354 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054718 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551269 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551250 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054719 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551277 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551245 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054758 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551252 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054771 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551284 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186054807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551267 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186055197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186055197 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3} + 1}$." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186055197 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186055197 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-1.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}+1,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1}{\\sqrt{3}-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{(1-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{3}-1)}{(\\sqrt{3}+1)(\\sqrt{3}-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(1)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-1-3+\\sqrt{3}}{3-1} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}-4}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{2} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061069 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061070 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061071 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061072 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186061073 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550527 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550555 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550582 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550884 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550988 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186061068 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550527 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064153 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551276 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064272 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551314 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064303 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551254 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551299 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064473 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064473 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $2.2.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186064473 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.5$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n\nLet $X_{1}, X_{2}, …, X_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n = 50$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $2.2$.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186064473 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.2+3\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+0.4+0.3+0.8 \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.2)-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.5+0.8+0.9+3.2)-4 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(2,\\frac{1.4}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(2,0.028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{2.2-2}{\\sqrt{0.028}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.20) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}>2.2)=1-Φ(1.20).\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068711 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551343 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068720 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551263 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551283 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068850 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551334 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068894 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551293 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551297 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186068926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551251 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186073742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551329 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186073788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551336 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186073882 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551238 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186075226 1078 #edb/ref 17592186075231 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078415 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551339 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078416 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551265 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078552 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551264 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078552 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551342 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078653 1050 420.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078725 1071 "A triangle $△LMN$ has $m∠L = 90^{∘}$, $m∠N = 37^{∘}$, and $LM = 8$. Find the length of $LN^(―)$ rounded to the nearest integer.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186078725 1071 "A triangle $△LMN$ has $m∠L={90}^{∘},$ $m∠N={37}^{∘},$ and $LM=8.$ Find the length of $\\overset{―}{LN}$ rounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186078725 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186080782 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550390 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186080782 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550390 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083203 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551300 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083203 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551308 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083204 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551260 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083312 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551257 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551236 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083365 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551341 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083366 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551280 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083367 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551324 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083631 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083631 1071 "$\\cos 60^{∘} + \\sin 90^{∘} =$ {{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186083631 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186083631 1074 "The angles of ${60}^{∘}$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}+1 & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186087710 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551270 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186092315 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551319 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186092316 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551304 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186092328 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551289 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186092329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551326 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186092329 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551335 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186096631 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551357 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186096632 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551235 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186097262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186095496 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186110620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551234 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113984 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113985 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113986 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113987 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186113988 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550509 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550776 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550785 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550915 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550957 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186113983 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550776 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551262 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551294 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115243 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551345 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551266 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551286 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115244 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551296 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551259 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115245 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551278 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115841 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115841 1071 "Find the product $E_{1}^{-1}E_{2}$, where\n$E_{1}|= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, E_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186115841 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186115841 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186116496 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186116496 1064 "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/01/0144b651cc6eff46265156e1491f58aacfc167da6dce571dbf4a92c9d94e7177.svg)\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/fb/fbb291c4f2d7ccc79939cf9368579b07f6649709f123fb70a1383deefaec6fc1.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n![](images/1e/1e71a52dda452f98fa77c01117f40d055fa0661100a0e4ffc25147d9baefc0af.svg)\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186119870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551247 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186119874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551271 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186119874 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551323 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186119875 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551268 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186119875 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551282 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186121211 1078 #edb/ref 17592186121213 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122078 1078 #edb/ref 17592186122082 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122133 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122134 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122135 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122136 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186122137 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550614 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550688 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550795 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550892 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550934 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186122132 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550614 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124284 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $(-3,-1]$\n\n- $(0,4]$\n\n- $[-2,2)$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124284 1071 "![](images/d9/d965e0b1087ad45206dd9259c963609f556bf5803af47ab18006bf40dffb2557.png)\n\n     \n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\nI. $(-3,-1]$\n\nII. $(0,4]$\n\nIII. $[-2,2)$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124285 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\n- $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\n- $x \\in (-1,4]$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124285 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-2|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nI. $x \\in (-\\infty, 2]$\n\nII. $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n\nIII. $x \\in (-1,4]$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124285 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124285 1074 "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-2|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:  \n\n1\\. Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $2$ units to the right,  to get $y= |x-2|.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y= |x-2|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-2|-2.$\n\n![](images/60/60c2601abfd3c4034991408f47b75c8cf70a1bccb72f73b2f9624a52d0ed7b42.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $x\\in(-\\infty,2].$  Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/45/45b0cdd26280d9379a449af41edbc9e69dd9d76f3b4116d938fbe708c21f6f29.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $x \\in (-1,4].$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/55/55b8483c59362d3a59dfd269db74f2c5f17a97cee1f25c896ece16890d80bd42.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124353 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport </b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vehicles</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Public Transport</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](images/f9/f91ceb948ae8dadf7a0ce4750d28e6550a996ce7adeb13e63d6622d5a273625b.svg)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124353 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year: \n\n- $35\\%$ of people who used their own vehicles swap to public transport. This implies that $65\\%$ of them remain using their own vehicles.\n\n- $25\\%$ of people who used public transport swap to their own vehicles. This implies that $75\\%$ of them remain using public transport.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **Vehicles** | **Public Transport ** |\n| To: | **Vehicles** | $0.65$ | $0.25$ |\n| **Public Transport** | $0.35$ | $0.75$ |  |\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n![](images/b2/b21fee1abe56dcfdc687be14278c62a20d201ff00e96f1e5918ce22d9dc8b03c.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124472 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551237 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124473 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551253 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124544 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551243 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124544 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551295 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124545 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551261 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124545 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551325 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551298 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124554 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551321 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551256 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551281 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124591 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551288 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551279 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124614 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551290 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124694 1050 420.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124711 1050 600.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124769 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124769 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.55 & 0.75\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124769 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$U = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, V = \\begin{bmatrix}0.45 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.55 & 0.75\\end{bmatrix}, W = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124769 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.2 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.55 & 0.75\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.45+0.55=1 \\\\ 0.25+0.75=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.5 & 0 & 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$V$ only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124913 1071 "There are three groups of fans in a certain town: those who prefer basketball, those who prefer baseball, and those who prefer football. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer basketball, $32%$ change to baseball and $28%$ change to football,\n\n- from the people that prefer baseball, $45%$ change to football and $15%$ change to basketball,\n\n- from the people that prefer football, $25%$ change to basketball and $65%$ change to baseball.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.15 & a \\\\ 0.32 & b & 0.65 \\\\ c & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124913 1071 "There are three groups of fans in a certain town: those who prefer basketball, those who prefer baseball, and those who prefer football. A study has shown that, each year\n\n- from the people that prefer basketball, $32%$ change to baseball and $28%$ change to football,\n- from the people that prefer baseball, $45%$ change to football and $15%$ change to basketball,\n- from the people that prefer football, $25%$ change to basketball and $65%$ change to baseball.\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a + b + c$?\n\n$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.15 & a \\\\ 0.32 & b & 0.65 \\\\ c & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124913 1074 "We are given the following situation for each year:\n\n- From the people who prefer basketball, $32%$ change to baseball and $28%$ change to football. This implies that $40%$ of them continue to prefer basketball.\n\n- From people who prefer baseball, $45%$ change to football and $15%$ change to basketball. This implies that $40%$ of them continue to prefer baseball.\n\n- From people who prefer football, $25%$ change to basketball and $65%$ change to baseball. This implies that $10%$ of them continue to prefer football.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Basketball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Baseball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Football</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Basketball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Baseball</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.65$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Football</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.15 & 0.25 \\\\ 0.32 & 0.4 & 0.65 \\\\ 0.28 & 0.45 & 0.1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.4+0.28=0.93.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124922 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{1}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186124922 1071 "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{bmatrix}, x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\\\ 0.2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector $x_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector $x_{1}$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186124922 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{1},$ we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.6 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.24 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.36\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186125800 1064 "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>City</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.94$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.03$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.06$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.97$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/aa/aa1be872e6f5cdcd842a2f02074f1b9ddd7619697973a2cd06a05f9c1cf9ab75.svg) \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n![](images/35/35a20015fc0e96a78ebf1ea2b8fa49721343279a39a35206d41c5ade0ac78ba8.svg)\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/93/93e30410bcea9b657efe84dc9c32e5f8b3fd3cff159c41f0196547ac5119c766.svg)\nThe resulting vector \n![](images/4b/4b331a70fadd7c061d20b85bb6a7c144de91880f386691f498b1b417f65fa264.svg)\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n![](images/53/53c774ea2bf14c3f98927d468db9d872c90d11bb8a7ce56f70f611e55faffab8.svg)\nThe resulting vector \n![](images/36/363f748dfe8d79023473d0e3a83f331ec6a92a905ddcc9115b675362a6194f1b.svg)\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186125800 1064 "We can use repeated matrix multiplication to determine the state of a system given its initial state and some information about how one state transitions to the next state.\n\nTo explain this concretely, suppose we have a population where people live either in the city or the countryside. A study carried out by the local authority shows that, each year,\n\n- $6\\%$ of the city population relocates to the countryside,\n\n- $3\\%$ of the countryside population relocates to the city, and\n\n- everyone else remains where they are.\n\nThis information can be represented using a diagram, like the one below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6b3cecac2af22b8665078db56c7daf492c0381434c9c88fe21f2630f743466.png)\n\nWe can also present this information as a table:\n\n|  |  | From: |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | **City** | **Countryside** |\n| To: | **City** | $0.94$ | $0.03$ |\n| **Countryside** | $0.06$ | $0.97$ |  |\n\nWe want to use this information to determine how the population will be distributed $n$ years after the study was carried out. \n\nFirst, let's turn this table into a matrix $P\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that this matrix has the following properties:\n\n- it is a square matrix\n\n- the sum of the entries in each column equals $1$\n\n- all entries in the matrix are non-negative\n\nA square matrix with these properties is called a **stochastic matrix**.\n\nNow suppose that, at the time of the study, $70\\%$ of the population lived in the city and $30\\%$ in the countryside. We can represent this **initial state** using a so-called **stochastic vector** $\\mathbf x_0,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStochastic vectors contain non-negative entries only, and the sum of the entries equals $1.$ Note that each column of $P$ is a stochastic vector.\n\nNow that we have our stochastic matrix and initial state vector, we can use matrix multiplication to calculate the population distribution sometime after the initial study took place:\n\n- To calculate the population distribution after one year, we calculate the product $P\\mathbf x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.667 \\\\ 0.333\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that one year after the study, $66.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $33.3$% will live in the countryside.\n\n- To model the distribution after $2$ years, we compute $P\\mathbf x_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting vector \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.637 \\\\ 0.363\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ntells us that two years after the study, $63.7$% of the population will live in the city, and $36.3$% will live in the countryside." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186125801 1064 "Let's reconsider the population we looked at earlier, where the stochastic vector that represents the initial distribution of people between the city and the countryside is\n\n![](images/a4/a423e2c13f55ff6af245ae6e51c7dfc6618d350f0e6de7ec813db176892be05c.svg)\nand the yearly change in this distribution is represented by the stochastic matrix \n\n![](images/71/714196a216ec63873059d772ce8c548ea644825f62ea32a6047469eaf285a875.svg)\n\nEarlier, we found that the population distribution after $1$ and $2$ years is given by the vectors $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ \n respectively, where\n\n![](images/d0/d0b99c9251cef4d82dd48b71d2f4bfc1884f43439a516bd007a1b8ea903c65a5.svg)\n\nand\n\n![](images/48/48982abc258980890c8afd48b2949401e33e2401f8c692e5b1c65b3c4c68705d.svg)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ are called **state vectors**, and $\\mathbf x_0$ is the **initial state**. \n\nTo find the state vector corresponding to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sequence of stochastic vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ related to a stochastic matrix $P$ in this way is called a **Markov chain**." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186125801 1064 "Let's reconsider the population we looked at earlier, where the stochastic vector that represents the initial distribution of people between the city and the countryside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.70 \\\\ 0.30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand the yearly change in this distribution is represented by the stochastic matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that the population distribution after $1$ and $2$ years is given by the vectors $\\mathbf x_1$ and $\\mathbf x_2,$ \n respectively, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0.94 & 0.03 \\\\ 0.06 & 0.97\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ are called **state vectors**, and $\\mathbf x_0$ is the **initial state**. \n\nTo find the state vector corresponding to the $(k+1)$th year, we left-multiply the state vector corresponding to the $k$th year by $P.$ This gives rise to the following sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1}, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =P{x}_{2}, \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮ \\\\ {x}_{k+1} & =P{x}_{k} \\\\ ⋮ & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA sequence of stochastic vectors $\\mathbf{x}_0, \\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots$ related to a stochastic matrix $P$ in this way is called a **Markov chain**." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186129117 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551356 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551303 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186129118 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551318 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551274 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186129120 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551355 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132374 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132375 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132376 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132377 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186132378 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550473 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550732 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550844 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550979 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551028 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186132373 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550732 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133616 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](images/03/0329ad2c30d00d6051e2be094d076960d3ecfc35f9054feac195787f3af7328d.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186133616 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression (in terms of $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$) for the vector of coefficients $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ of the curve $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2 + \\beta_3 x^3$ that best fits the data. \n\n![](images/17/172f8147d4d58d4d3edc42f96e3fb248f59cb749ed0e387c9c8bcddfec08bf79.svg)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551291 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133804 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551310 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551239 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133805 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551337 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133806 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551244 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133807 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551292 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551340 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551305 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551332 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551350 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186134029 1050 540.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186134030 1050 420.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186134389 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186134389 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186134389 1071 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x + β_{2}x^{2} + β_{3}x^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1} \\\\ β_{2} \\\\ β_{3}\\end{bmatrix} = (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y$,\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186134389 1074 "| $x$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2} & {5}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186135062 1064 "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1} &=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1} &=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1} &=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1} &=15\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 100 \\\\ 1 & 110 \\\\ 1 & 120 \\\\ 1 & 130\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186135062 1064 "Consider the following data set:\n\n| $x$ | **$100$** | **$110$** | **$120$** | **$130$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $12$ | $11$ | $12$ | $15$ |\n\nOur goal is to describe how to use matrices to carry out a linear regression. That is, we want to find an equation of the form\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points. \n\n![](images/8e/8ec830e3cf6154675487536afc0e09a42226eb83187b90238a0bfdcefeae5b38.png)\n\nLet's write our problem in matrix notation. Substituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+100{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+110{\\beta }_{1}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+120{\\beta }_{1}=12 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+130{\\beta }_{1}=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be expressed in matrix notation as\n\n![](images/8b/8bacbea6990395351aecca8befe779358d57d50f8c14f865d73c2107688fa728.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **design matrix,** and $\\mathbf{y}$ is called the **observation vector**. \n\nSince our points do not lie on a straight line, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0$ and $\\beta_1$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nThis approach to finding parameters for a linear regression model is often called **ordinary least squares (OLS) regression**.\n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta }=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the line of best fit (regression line) is \n\n$$\ny = 1+ 0.1x.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186135063 1064 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nIn particular, this would mean that the sum of the squared residuals [math]\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2[/math] must be as small as possible.\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=15 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+4{\\beta }_{1}+{4}^{2}{\\beta }_{2} &=18\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThis system can be written using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. \n\nNow, using the matrix notation, the sum of the squared residuals can be viewed as \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^4 r_i^2 = \\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2.\n\\]\n\nThus, the condition that this sum must be minimized is equivalent to minimizing [math]\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|^2[/math] or, which is the same, minimizing [math]\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\beta\\|.[/math]\n\nSince our points do not lie on a quadratic curve, there will be no parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ such that this equation is true. So instead, we seek parameters $\\beta_0, \\beta_1,$ and $\\beta_2$ that best approximate the solution.\n\nIn other words, we seek to find a column vector $\\boldsymbol\\beta$ such that\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf{y} - X\\boldsymbol{\\beta}\\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible.\n\nNotice that this is exactly the least-squares problem for the system $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}.$ Therefore, the coefficients of the curve that best fits the data can be found as the solution to the corresponding least-squares problem! \n\nPre-multiplying the equation $X \\boldsymbol{\\beta} = \\mathbf{y}$ by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible, and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $X$ and $\\mathbf{y}$ into the formula above and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta }=\\begin{bmatrix}22.5 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the quadratic curve of best fit (regression curve) is \n\n$$\ny = 22.5 - 9x + 2x^2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186135063 1064 "We've seen how to construct a simple linear regression model given some bivariate data $(x,y).$ We can generalize this technique to fit data to an arbitrary polynomial. We call this process **polynomial regression**.\n\nThe following table gives the number of units $x$ (in hundreds) produced by a small company and the respective profits $y$ (in thousands of dollars) earned for producing $x$ units:\n\n| Goods $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $16$ | $11$ | $15$ | $18$ |\n\nSuppose we want to find the quadratic function\n\n$$\ny = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x + \\beta_2 x^2\n$$\n\nthat best fits the given data points.\n\n![](images/93/93fe1784b89ce4c892c84a03a0dc80909f16f46ac463c5cc30b40f869a7802bf.png)\n\nSubstituting the values of $x$ into the regression equation and equating the results to the corresponding observed values of $y,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0}+1{\\beta }_{1}+{1}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=16 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+2{\\beta }_{1}+{2}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=11 \\\\ {\\beta }_{0}+3{\\beta }_{1}+{3}^{2}{\\beta }_{2}=15 \\\\ {\\bet