 }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186157711 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|1-(-7)|,|-2-8|) \\\\  & =max(8,10) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158002 1071 "In a particular park, the smallest tree is $6$ meters tall, and the tallest tree $10$ meters tall. Assuming that the height of the trees is uniformly distributed, what is the mean height of the trees in this park?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158002 1071 "In a particular park, the smallest tree is $6$ meters tall, and the tallest tree $10$ meters tall. Assuming that the height of the trees is uniformly distributed, what is the mean height of the trees in this park?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186158002 1074 "Let $X$ represent the height of a randomly chosen tree. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[6,10],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[6,10],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{6+10}{2}=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean height of the trees is $8\\,\\text{m}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158008 1071 "The duration of a podcast episode is uniformly distributed between $25$ and $34$ minutes. What is the expected duration of an episode?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186158008 1071 "The duration of a podcast episode is uniformly distributed between $25$ and $34$ minutes. What is the expected duration of an episode?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186158008 1074 "Let $X$ represent the duration of a randomly chosen episode. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[25,34],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[25,34],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{25+34}{2}=29.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean duration of an episode is $29.5\\,\\text{min}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161211 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161212 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161213 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161214 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186161215 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550447 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550501 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550502 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550764 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551037 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161210 1078 #edb/ref 17592186551037 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161797 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/09/09d935cd479371e7cde4e52b2073cb23343a5137f311803c8da5879a59896fb4.svg)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/51/51694be12d594a39b6a07f48b37aedb47323fc5537a60f5d6a766576103d48f6.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186161797 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/44/44753acffd8b6384cd291f9b0758edd7676acb319caa22d5b07be36dcf72a5a6.png)\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/21/217b5d7d3b82800bdfc44d83ca8b068e02a37759e52b7a8382505bfb102f753f.png)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 + 2y_2^2 - y_3^2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186162005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551346 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162005 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551351 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551306 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162007 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551327 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162221 1050 840.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162653 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x) = 9x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} + 6x_{2}^{2}$, find its canonical (diagonal) form $y^{T}Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x = Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186162653 1071 "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+6{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186162653 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}9-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-15\\lambda +50 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-10I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186163229 1064 "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/43/43e0dc28161c0564a75f6ea2dc7f0e09ad74dffae05b864e13c61330221f2983.svg)\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n![](images/c9/c93cabd6a18df429b5aad99815b23e337c17b88a1c271dd17344631dfc5f8ebb.svg)\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186163229 1064 "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186166920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551313 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186166920 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551331 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186175910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551287 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186175923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551333 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186179739 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551246 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186179742 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551240 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551248 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551312 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551320 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184142 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551344 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551255 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551273 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551317 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551328 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184226 1038 #edb/ref 17592186551353 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186184422 1050 660.0 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186185347 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}u & =1-{sin}^{2}u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{sin}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186185347 1064 "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203419 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203420 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550969 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203420 1078 #edb/ref 17592186550969 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203421 1075 #edb/ref 17592186551024 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203421 1078 #edb/ref 17592186551024 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186203422 1075 #edb/ref 17592186550949 13194139535975 1759218604541