width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$42$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$18$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186204581 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{1}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{y}_{2}}({y}_{1}{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ {y}_{2} & {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=y({x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\ 1+2+3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{3}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{3}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}{x}_{2}{x}_{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{3}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}{x}_{3} & {x}_{1}{x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 42 & 24 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186204589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186204589 1071 "Consider the functions $y: R^{2} → R^{2}$ and $f: R^{2} → R^{2}$, defined by\n$y(x) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}(x_{1}^{2} + x_{2}^{2}) \\\\ x_{1} + x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}, f(y) = \\begin{bmatrix}y_{1}y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{2} + y_{2}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat is\n$\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x))$ evaluated at\n$x_{0} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$?\n\n| $\\left.\\frac{d}{dx}f(y(x))\\right\\|_{x_{0}} =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $4$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |" 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186204589 1071 "Consider the functions $y:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ and $f:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2},$ defined b