n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus, the total derivative of $y$ with respect to $x$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{1}}{∂{x}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{y}_{2}}{∂{x}_{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}(\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2})) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂{x}_{2}}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & {x}_{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 4 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186220830 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220831 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186220833 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220834 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186220836 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220838 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186220839 1078 #edb/ref 17592186220841 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186220900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220901 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186220900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220902 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186220900 1075 #edb/ref 17592186220903 13194139535975 17592186045417 false] [17592186220