 #edb/ref 17592186551397 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551413 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551432 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551442 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551453 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551455 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551458 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1084 #edb/ref 17592186551468 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1085 "![](images/4f/4f2a3229336aa53a36621138c9cee114b1dee0c8244d778a6e3e2ac28950e908.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1086 #uuid "f86c67b8-5ef3-50e6-9f71-b4614feb6dde" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551232 1087 #edb/ref 17592186551413 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551062 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551070 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551135 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551173 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1069 "q-331789" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1071 "Consider a function $r(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{r(t)\\}=R(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1072 #uuid "002b6494-db6e-5c52-baa6-f8349498d2c1" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551233 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{3t}r(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{3t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $R(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s-3)t}\\,r(t)\\,\\text{d}t=R(s-3),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns-3>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}+3.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551213 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1069 "q-14753" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1071 "Solve for $C$ where $\\frac{C-4}{3}=\\frac{C-2}{6}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1072 #uuid "0a65343e-809a-580c-9503-c6f1ca221068" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551234 1074 "First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{C-4}{3} & =\\frac{C-2}{6} \\\\ (C-4)\\cdot 6 & =3\\cdot (C-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(C-4)\\cdot 6 & =3\\cdot (C-2) \\\\ 6C-24 & =3C-6 \\\\ 6C-24-3C & =3C-6-3C \\\\ 3C-24 & =-6 \\\\ 3C-24+24 & =-6+24 \\\\ 3C & =18 \\\\ \\frac{3C}{3} & =\\frac{18}{3} \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C=6.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551067 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1069 "q-114899" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{2}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1072 #uuid "0eabe319-fbfe-5f66-832c-db699322266a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551235 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=2.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-2}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,  ${x}_{k}=2+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(2+\\frac{k}{n})}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)=4{x}^{3}$ and $b=3.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551188 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1069 "q-5994" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1071 "$3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1072 #uuid "0f16c2b9-1c8b-50a4-93ab-bbcc2578659f" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551236 1074 "The angles ${30}^{∘}$ and ${45}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have the  values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nAlso, note that ${tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}$ simply means $(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{tan}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ 3{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘})}^{2}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘} & = \\\\ 3{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}-1 & = \\\\ 3(\\frac{3}{9})-1 & = \\\\ 1-1 & = \\\\ 0 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551156 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1069 "q-108578" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1071 "![](images/44/448ae88055ea0da5dd44fb3db608b5ac71dbd3709c88edd41440c15124d74eae.png)\n\nWhich of the graphs above correspond to an invertible function?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1072 #uuid "0fa77d6e-f50d-59b7-b662-8c42e4bce6dc" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551237 1074 "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](images/e5/e582f5233163be3a094ac575ba0cf2a37b417038be8894a12717ca31d401e4d6.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- For graphs I, III, and IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graphs I, III, and IV show one-to-one functions. As a result, those functions are invertible.\n\n- For graph II, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, this is not a one-to-one function. As a result, it is not invertible." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551093 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1069 "q-92838" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1071 "The ratio of the width of a volleyball court to its length is $\\frac{1}{2}.$ If the length of the volleyball court is $18$ meters, what is its width?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1072 #uuid "14d57c7d-ff6e-530b-b67b-f43cfc293439" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551238 1074 "The ratio of width to length is $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the width and the denominator represents the length.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $18$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $9$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1\\times 9}{2\\times 9}=\\frac{9}{18}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the width of the volleyball court is $9$ meters." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551139 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1069 "q-41739" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1071 "A physics experiment consists of a small object moving along a straight line. The following table contains data collected on the distance traveled by the object (in inches) during the first five seconds. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Distance $(y)$ | $8.6$ | $13.1$ | $24.6$ | $28.1$ | $38.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what distance will the object cover after $6$ seconds, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1072 #uuid "1515bb33-4938-5f32-8710-f2dc1f06ddad" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551181 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1069 "q-125497" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1071 "![](images/4e/4ee9e02184efd314371a99c6242540e5e2e6effc9e21d41a86648b433b8a6938.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1072 #uuid "17a8497e-763c-5804-a8ea-e63d106be3fe" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551240 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d91a85ccbb948e070db738373ffc5032f4e91a0ba8e04cd14e79276b0e798f8.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8e0bffd04fb7b765d3c654f62b1958ab55a42f0d26cbc9f2b2e31f7d8e6f775.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551185 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1069 "q-318203" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1071 "When a new app was first launched, it had $60$ downloads. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of downloads increased by $120%$ of the number of downloads at the end of the previous day.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $D$ is the estimated number of downloads $t$ days after the app was launched, and $t\\le 7?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1072 #uuid "1818fb31-e6dd-51ed-98a5-48141bc40666" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551241 1074 "The number of downloads $D(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=60$ is the total number of downloads initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nD=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $D(t)$ is\n\n$$\nD=60\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nD(t)=60\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551160 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1069 "q-254896" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1071 "Suppose $a=[3,\\,2]^{T},$ and $b=[1,\\,0]^{T},$ and $p=3.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1072 #uuid "1902a701-3e4f-5245-87f9-f7a41d88dc2e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551242 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{3}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]‖}_{3} \\\\  & ={(|3-1{|}^{3}+|2-0{|}^{3})}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={16}^{1/3} \\\\  & ≈2.520.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551128 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1069 "q-135555" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1071 "In a particular country, there are two groups of people: those who live in the cities and those who live in the countryside. A study has shown that each decade \n\n- $12%$ of people who lived in the cities relocate to the countryside, and\n\n- $28%$ of people who lived in the countryside relocate to the cities.\n\nWhich of the following tables describes how these people change their preferences each year?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1072 #uuid "1cf7f6b8-f438-52c0-a338-61f2ad7fa03e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551243 1074 "We are given the following situation for each decade: \n\n- $12%$ of people who live in the cities relocate to the countryside. This implies that $88%$ of them remain living in the cities.\n\n- $28%$ of people who live in the countryside relocate to the cities. This implies that $72%$ of them remain living in the countryside.\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Cities</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.88$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.28$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Countryside</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.12$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNote that we can write a corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.88 & 0.28 \\\\ 0.12 & 0.72\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551214 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1069 "q-139660" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1072 #uuid "1dfba625-81c0-581d-a463-4b6d422c9f73" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551244 1074 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {1}^{2} & {1}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {3}^{2} & {3}^{3} \\\\ 1 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 14 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551200 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1069 "q-111093" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1072 #uuid "20eb9dff-e6a3-54ea-a4e3-f227327ba30b" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551245 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})}{(\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{2})(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})}{3-2} & = \\\\ 3(\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551115 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1069 "q-125490" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1071 "![](images/9f/9f94742a7f37fd7f6a1c789401a2e323fec80aca584774453cba03cf69f28ade.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path along the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1072 #uuid "2310204a-fdf5-57d1-9e96-98189fedc070" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551246 1074 "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4e8c62562ee1657e41bb36a21c901dc95935558641f724f934a4c58706f4a64.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/40/40b100dd4bc71b95f5ad99eb0cec8ed87133211bb371b22deccb851f79c695b3.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551101 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1069 "q-42916" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1071 "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries below ${a}_{14}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1072 #uuid "231b9e48-9ef0-506b-a5a1-fc73da681086" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551247 1074 "Using elementary row operations, let's make entries below ${a}_{14}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -13 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 6\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 \\\\ 9 & -7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(35-(-9)) \\\\  & =-264.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551143 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1069 "q-274506" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1072 #uuid "24a538ad-232b-5718-acdd-ce07c94cc6d5" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551248 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{36}-\\frac{25}{36} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})+(-\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{5}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}}{18}-\\frac{10}{18} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{55}-10}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551060 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1069 "q-44435" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1071 "Which of the following shows a plot of the line $x=-1?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1072 #uuid "26fb6f7e-8d80-56e2-95a0-d9d21d4fb0e3" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551249 1074 "On the line $x=-1,$ every point has an $x$-coordinate of $-1.$\n\nSo, the plot of $x=-1$ is the following vertical line:\n\n![](images/00/00b0a9c54b56b91417cb89752b39b1d5969ca631cc56da84815a0bb5d4cbead3.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551142 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1069 "q-124392" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1071 "![](images/e5/e597e317f32a2a9a3757ce583a2b053f4cba1da1a2119c572671e7edc96c61e4.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{6}{7},\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1072 #uuid "29930a9c-51d8-56d8-87b6-15535f5105d7" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551250 1074 "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7})}{(\\frac{6}{7})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{6}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551217 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1069 "q-167103" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1072 #uuid "2ac6642f-2218-5fe7-be53-d9c45b78af41" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551251 1074 "Previously, we found that $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has equation\n\n$$\ny=-2x+5.\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ 0 & =-2x+5 \\\\ 2x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=\\frac{5}{2}.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(\\frac{5}{2},0).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551096 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1069 "q-293483" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1071 "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3}}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1072 #uuid "2be8c3b4-39c9-5644-83d2-b15c8e4f70a2" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551252 1074 "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{1(\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3})} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{7-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551105 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1069 "q-36939" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1071 "![](images/c2/c25885ba540cda475b528516df948ae3a7547c91ca78a8dcf2034284c50a4ca6.png)\n\nDetermine the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ (shown above) is invertible." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1072 #uuid "2c532f18-d5b7-5299-a745-04db386fb5cd" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551253 1074 "First, notice that the domain of our function is $x\\in [-4,4].$ \n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ does not pass the horizontal line test on the interval $x\\in (-1,1).$ In this case, the horizontal line $y=1$ intersects the graph at more than one point.\n\n![](images/d2/d295d104bd8340a8701327e19103bfa8fd12622f1500c405411d2afd8bd47801.png)\n\n- Among the given options, the largest domain on which the function $f(x)$ is invertible is $x\\in [-4,-1)\\cup [0,4].$ On this domain, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/a7/a74a9a3f1fe52a7c841c763ccffb51faa922fdaec56825ef1000b5becc3534fe.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x\\in [-4,-1)\\cup [0,4].$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551122 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1069 "q-2973" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1071 "$\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}}$ is equivalent to" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1072 #uuid "2d3bb2ee-cb7d-5b52-9b6d-3169a083b4de" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551254 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent, and then we apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[5]{{(4b)}^{2}} & = \\\\ {({(4b)}^{2})}^{1/5} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2\\cdot (1/5)} & = \\\\ {(4b)}^{2/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551119 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1069 "q-326306" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf $v$ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=${{field-1}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1072 #uuid "2e61a3ba-7f7b-576c-9651-31afc3ef6f71" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551255 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{25}-\\frac{9}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the second quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(-\\frac{3}{5})+(\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{65}+\\frac{48}{65} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{65}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551227 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1069 "q-94616" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{5}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $5,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{5},$ where $a\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{5}[t]$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1072 #uuid "31d0899c-0442-5601-b2c5-58d91ebde08a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551256 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{5}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{5},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & ={a}_{1}{t}^{5}+{a}_{2}{t}^{5} \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{5}$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =ka{t}^{5} \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{5},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551172 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1069 "q-1802" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1071 "Evaluate $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3}).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1072 #uuid "31fc7ad4-02c1-5d63-bb54-860ef7ba25ac" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551257 1074 "First, note that $(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{7}{3})$ means the same thing as ${}_{7}{C}_{3}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{7!}{(7-3)!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7!}{4!3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{\\cancel{4!}3!} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 6\\cdot 5}{6} \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551094 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551107 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551193 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1069 "q-344058" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1072 #uuid "336eb7d8-27df-584e-adc9-df5d1de8bc4e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551258 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{9}\\cdot (-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551136 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1069 "q-130818" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1072 #uuid "3433ea61-fe1b-529a-a624-2ade0d70ec66" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551259 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551167 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1069 "q-73818" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1071 "$\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1072 #uuid "343c6002-3a70-5903-a244-0acddb5859ce" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551260 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}$ using synthetic division.\n\nFor synthetic division to work, we need the coefficient of $x$ in the denominator to be equal to $1.$ So first, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5} & =\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3(x-\\frac{5}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process on $\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}$, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & 9 & 12 & 0 & 0 & -25 \\\\  & ↓ & 15 & 45 & 75 & 125 \\\\  & 9 & 27 & 45 & 75 & 100\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})} & =\\frac{1}{3}(9{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}+45x+75+\\frac{100}{(x-\\frac{5}{3})}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{9{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}-25}{3x-5}=3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}+15x+25+\\frac{100}{3x-5}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551184 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1069 "q-35847" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1071 "A certain company produces $2$ types of flour, flour $A$ and flour $B.$ The following table describes how the number of consumers changes between both types of flour each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"2\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"2\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $A$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Flour $B$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $70%$ of flour $A$ customers switch to flour $B$ each month, and $60%$ of flour $B$ customers change to flour $A.$\n\nCurrently, $6\\,000$ customers use flour $A,$ while $4\\,000$ customers use flour $B.$ How many customers will use flour $A$ in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1072 #uuid "34952883-ed67-58e1-83a4-6ee369a6dde8" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551261 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of flour is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(10\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{10\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}6\\,000 \\\\ 4\\,000\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one year, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 & 0.6 \\\\ 0.7 & 0.4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.6 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.42 \\\\ 0.58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $42%$ of customers will use flour $A.$ Computing $42%$ of $10\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n10\\,000\\cdot 0.42=4\\,200.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4\\,200$ customers will use flour $A$ in one month." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551171 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1069 "q-42754" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,X=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1072 #uuid "3a9d38f9-b939-5805-88eb-ef96ffddb725" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551262 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551130 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1069 "q-167099" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1071 "What is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$\n\n$|\\overset{―}{AB}|\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1072 #uuid "3b67bc82-d512-510a-bcaa-06aed32ef341" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551263 1074 "From the picture, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3),$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{―}{AB}| & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-4)^{2}+(3-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551074 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1069 "q-122322" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1071 "![](images/9c/9ceef67f02c022065be31de7ec1b6785d270c194aee29c4d0a20707998932134.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{BC}$ rounded to one decimal place." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1072 #uuid "3c476ad6-03e3-57c6-ac0c-7d3e461a358b" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551264 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{BC}{12} \\\\ BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =9.456\\,12… \\\\  & ≈9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551068 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1069 "q-78669" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4x-3q$, where $q$ is a constant." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1072 #uuid "3d4bf850-5813-5f60-9b39-8cfc55a16cca" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551265 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=4x-3q\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=4y-3q\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4y-3q \\\\ x+3q & =4y \\\\ \\frac{x+3q}{4} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3q}{4}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551168 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1069 "q-130784" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$L=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1072 #uuid "3df63019-7de5-5b3e-afc6-d7a9e953df1d" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551266 1074 "We write down $L$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551064 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551226 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1069 "q-167097" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1071 "What are the $xy$-coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n$A\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1072 #uuid "403888bf-8094-5cf9-8e20-26739c8d4dcf" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551267 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$- and $y$-axes.\n\n* The point is $4$ units to the *right* of the origin, in the *positive* direction of the $x$-axis. So, its $x$-coordinate is $4.$\n\n* The point is $3$ units *below* the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $y$-axis. So, its $y$-coordinate is $-3.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of our point are $(4,-3).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551089 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1069 "q-91219" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1072 #uuid "4038f08b-56e1-5740-bb03-adc638c1a6b4" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551268 1074 "Let's make entries to the left and to the right of ${a}_{13}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -1 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+(-1){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-3){C}_{3} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+4{C}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 9 \\\\ 6 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551197 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1069 "q-267389" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1071 "![](images/fe/fe2f1266db9f675a2c82803728d37f53d0995c25cf61f74304a731c3d21c64c8.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},\\frac{5}{6})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1072 #uuid "44100483-c880-5325-8cb2-5b4178bd1ae8" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551269 1074 "![](images/48/489a919487ffe7051a27f736ff8ca210e4c246bceac42055e31f5150bc4e094b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551063 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551075 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1069 "q-167104" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1071 "Write the equation of $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ in **standard form**.\n\nThe equation is \n{{field-1}} \n$x+y=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1072 #uuid "44ed6b9e-67d9-570a-adb5-f3b8825b1118" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551270 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+5 \\\\ 2x+y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\n2x+y=5.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551125 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1069 "q-42930" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above and below ${a}_{33}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1072 #uuid "45127938-c6a2-5a13-8b44-5358b2433862" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551271 1074 "Let's make entries above and below ${a}_{33}=-1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+{R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{3} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $3$rd column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -7 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 7 & 8\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551059 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1069 "q-254906" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1071 "Suppose $a=[4,\\,3,\\,1]^{T},$ and $b=[5,\\,3,\\,4]^{T},$ and $p=4.$ Find the Minkowski distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\nRounded to three decimal places, ${d}_{p}(a,b)≈$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1072 #uuid "462b7d51-6045-5919-bcf3-708ab4590d36" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551272 1074 "Recall that the Minkowski distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{p}(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{p}\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{p}={({\\sum }_{i=1}^{N}|{v}_{i}{|}^{p})}^{1/p}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{4}(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]‖}_{4} \\\\  & ={(|4-5{|}^{4}+|3-3{|}^{4}+|1-4{|}^{4})}^{1/4} \\\\  & ={82}^{1/4} \\\\  & ≈3.009.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551120 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1069 "q-141389" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{3}{4},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a\\sqrt{3}-b\\sqrt{7}}{8},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1072 #uuid "4643f6f8-6def-57fc-9f05-9106c1781f57" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551273 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡v=1-{sin}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{4})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{8}-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}-1\\sqrt{7}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=3,b=1,$ and $a+b=4.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551110 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1069 "q-38072" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1071 "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4x+3 \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+11x+11\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+11x+11 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3x+11 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3x+6}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+11x+11}{x+2}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1072 #uuid "47e4cafc-245d-597a-859f-2f903cb45c89" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551274 1074 "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4x+3 \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+11x+11\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}+11x+11 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}+8x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3x+11 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{3x+6}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial ${x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+11x+11$ by the linear binomial $x+2$ gives\n\n- the quotient ${x}^{2}+4x+3,$ and\n\n- the remainder $5.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+11x+11}{x+2}={x}^{2}+4x+3+\\frac{5}{x+2}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551099 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1069 "q-96951" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1071 "A publisher has noticed that the minimum time needed to review a book is $115$ working days, and the maximum time is $235$ days. Assuming that the review time is uniformly distributed, what is the variance of the time needed to review a book?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1072 #uuid "48548786-5710-5326-90a5-450f466b1ddc" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551275 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time needed to review a randomly chosen book. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[115,235],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[115,235],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(235-115)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{14400}{12}=1200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the reviewing time is $1200\\,{\\text{days}}^{2}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551106 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1069 "q-112093" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1071 "In a factory, the cost of producing an item depends on the number of items produced per day. The function $U(x)$ models the cost, in dollars, to produce an item if the factory is producing $x$ items per day. What is the best interpretation of ${U}^{′}(500)=0.01?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1072 #uuid "4fdd627b-10f3-56fb-8e8b-56c0cb38ac41" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551276 1074 "The function ${U}^{′}(x)$ represents the rate at which the cost changes when the factory produces $x$ items per day. \n\nTherefore, ${U}^{′}(500)=0.01$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *When the factory produces $500$ items per day, the cost per item increases at a rate of $0.01$ dollars per item*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551155 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1069 "q-267419" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1071 "![](images/a8/a8fc1b09d51bf0d7ac526a917b2341015b787b3098627bedd25e1638ecf041cc.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1072 #uuid "5237ac2b-249d-5de5-a0f0-312a03200ea8" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551277 1074 "![](images/fd/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551090 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1069 "q-130816" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1071 "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1072 #uuid "5238d337-5418-5670-995a-6cacdf9c34e1" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551278 1074 "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551151 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1069 "q-135481" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic vectors?\n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.1 \\\\ 0.9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1072 #uuid "55574236-41f2-5d82-abc0-f140064784e6" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551279 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our vectors in turn.\n\n- Vector $u$ is *not*  a stochastic vector since the entries do not add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0.5=0.5\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- Vector $v$ is *not* a stochastic vector since it contains negative entries.\n\n- Vector $w$ is a stochastic vector. Indeed, \n\n- its entries are all nonnegative, and\n\n- the entries add up to one:\n\n$$\n0+0.1+0.9=1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$w$ only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551194 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1069 "q-28935" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1071 "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1072 #uuid "602ff191-9fc8-5da7-a56d-e21a1e6c8aee" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551280 1074 "The angles ${45}^{∘},$ ${30}^{∘}$ and ${0}^{∘}$ are special angles, and their trigonometric ratios have the values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=0.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551220 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1069 "q-107105" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{3}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $3,$ and its subset $B=\\{a{t}^{3}+b{t}^{2}+ct\\,|\\,a,b,c\\in Q\\}.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $B$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $B$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $B$ is a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$\n\nIV. $B$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{3}[t]$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1072 #uuid "60c155e6-f24c-5a27-86a4-e2d2fb148ceb" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551281 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{3}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{1}t$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{3}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}t$ are two polynomials from $B,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{3}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{1}t)+({a}_{2}{t}^{3}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}t) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{3}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}){t}^{2}+({c}_{1}+{c}_{2})t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $B$ since ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},\\,{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}$ are in $Q.$ So, $B$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $f(t)={t}^{2}$ and $k=\\sqrt{2},$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not a polynomial from $B,$ because $\\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$ So, $B$ is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false, while statement IV is true. $B$ is *not* a subspace because it's not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551202 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1069 "q-132023" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{41}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1072 #uuid "634c0345-e97a-5738-8ae3-640a8e78ce93" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551282 1074 "Let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{41}=1$ equal to zero using elementary column operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 7 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 9 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 13 & -9 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+1}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 13 & -9 & 11\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551163 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1069 "q-3479" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1071 "What is $\\frac{3t+6}{4t+8}$ reduced to its lowest terms?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1072 #uuid "6410f1b3-233a-5db4-a768-91ae7f99b09a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551283 1074 "We factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any factors that they have in common.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3t+6}{4t+8} & =\\frac{3\\cdot (t+2)}{4\\cdot (t+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{(t+2)}}{4\\cdot \\cancel{(t+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551112 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1069 "q-154008" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1071 "Solve the inequality $-\\frac{y}{3}\\le 3.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1072 #uuid "66604494-d5d9-5eb8-81e3-a76e00656f7f" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551284 1074 "The variable $y$ is being *divided* by $3.$ To isolate $y$ and remove the negative sign, we *multiply* by $-3.$ \n\nRemember, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{3}\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,(-3)\\cdot 3 \\\\ (-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3})\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two negative numbers multiply to give a positive number, we first cancel the negatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3)\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ (\\cancel{-}3)\\cdot (\\cancel{-}\\frac{y}{3}) & \\ge -9 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $3$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\frac{y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{3y}{3} & \\ge -9 \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}y}{\\cancel{3}} & \\ge -9 \\\\ y & \\ge -9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y\\ge -9.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551166 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551190 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551206 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551215 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1069 "q-342257" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-5x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1072 #uuid "6acce05c-927e-54a3-ab7e-dea3332ed930" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551285 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/64/64b557365c3642ae7a4206794080804bf2bbccb07230dfa46a4f415570f85329.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}-5x=-5(-2)=10\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(2)^{2}=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{true}$ since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd37979eada411b68755dfa85ec3b1e22585df2bff5c4bdd4b27cf06463706d6.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551224 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1069 "q-130788" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$N=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1072 #uuid "6ad7a8de-9677-5fd6-a871-171f20886f2e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551286 1074 "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551083 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1069 "q-167105" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(4,0)$ and is parallel to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1072 #uuid "6b070545-3ae6-5e49-a6d2-101dac09a1fa" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551287 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-2.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-2$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,0).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-2\\cdot (x-4) \\\\ y & =-2x+8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551164 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1069 "q-107100" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1071 "Consider the vector space ${R}_{4}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $4,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2},$ with $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{4}[t]$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1072 #uuid "6c7a58a4-6c9f-528f-bd18-00fe71c43a41" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551288 1074 "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}{t}^{4}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}{t}^{4}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}{t}^{4}+{b}_{1}{t}^{2})+({a}_{2}{t}^{4}+{b}_{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2}){t}^{4}+({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}){t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2}\\in A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(a{t}^{4}+b{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =(ka){t}^{4}+(kb){t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551169 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1069 "q-111383" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $5$, what is the highest possible degree of $P+Q?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1072 #uuid "70cb115a-1a5a-5899-8751-fbde76c6f208" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551289 1074 "The highest possible degree of $P+Q$ is $5.$\n\nFor example, suppose $P={x}^{5}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $P+Q=2{x}^{5}.$ \n\nSince neither $P$ nor $Q$ contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5},$ $P+Q$ can't contain terms of order higher than ${x}^{5}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551158 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1069 "q-135486" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1071 "Which of the following are stochastic matrices?\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{aligned}],\\,W=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1072 #uuid "718b908f-f156-56c4-954e-a24b1b0d0bf9" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551290 1074 "Recall that a stochastic vector is a vector with nonnegative entries that add up to $1.$ A matrix is stochastic if all its columns are stochastic vectors.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices in turn.\n\n- Matrix $U$ is a stochastic matrix. Indeed, each column is a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries are nonnegative and add up to one): \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0.1 & 0.7 \\\\ 0.9 & 0.3\\end{aligned}]\\,\\begin{aligned}0.1+0.9=1 \\\\ 0.7+0.3=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Matrix $V$ is *not* a stochastic matrix since its first column is not a stochastic vector (the corresponding entries don't add up to one): \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0.45 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.25 & 0.65\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Matrix $W$  *not* a stochastic matrix since its second column is not a stochastic vector (it contains negative entries):\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ only.\"" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551111 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1069 "q-139644" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1071 "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-8$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1072 #uuid "776ef62d-2c09-5e2b-a7f2-e7cbc949af5b" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551291 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=-3-6x.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551176 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1069 "q-139599" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2} & {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1072 #uuid "789ea0d3-54ac-52af-9aa3-c806bd076e69" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551292 1074 "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551179 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1069 "q-167100" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1071 "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1072 #uuid "79bc14d7-179c-5c17-ba5e-232b6f91a6cf" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551293 1074 "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(4,-3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,3),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-3)}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{-3} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551222 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1069 "q-42756" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1071 "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,N=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1072 #uuid "7abe0f71-31aa-5c86-a7a0-fa2df71ee9ab" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551294 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551146 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1069 "q-135564" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1071 "There are three supermarket chains in a particular city: chain $A,$ chain $B,$ and chain $C.$ A study has shown that, each month\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5%$ change to chain $B$ and $15%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25%$ change to chain $A$ and $25%$ change to chain $C,$\n\n- from the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15%$ change to chain $A$ and $35%$ change to chain $B.$\n\nThe stochastic matrix that describes this situation is given below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & a & 0.15 \\\\ b & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & c\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1072 #uuid "7d2bfda5-1f22-5eca-90c5-42502b97859c" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551295 1074 "We are given the following situation for each month:\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $A,$ $5%$ change to chain $B$ and $15%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $80%$ of them continue to prefer chain $A.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $B,$ $25%$ change to chain $A$ and $25%$ change to chain $C.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer chain $B.$\n\n- From the customers that prefer chain $C,$ $15%$ change to chain $A$ and $35%$ change to chain $B.$ This implies that $50%$ of them continue to prefer chain $C.$\n\nWriting this information in tabular form, we obtain the following:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain A</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain B</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.05$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"80\"><b>Chain C</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe can write the corresponding stochastic matrix as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.8 & 0.25 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.05 & 0.5 & 0.35 \\\\ 0.15 & 0.25 & 0.5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c=0.25+0.05+0.5=0.8.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551102 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1069 "q-32008" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1071 "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$S=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1072 #uuid "7effc142-a727-5443-a7a6-2e2db67c4b1f" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551296 1074 "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551087 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551149 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1069 "q-167101" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1071 "Find the $y$-intercept of $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ and express the equation of this line in slope-intercept form.\n\nThe $y$ - intercept of the line is $b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1072 #uuid "7f09f62f-f5bc-5004-8782-5f89d18c5383" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551297 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nAs we found previously, the slope is $m=-2.$\n\nSubstituting $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ -3 & =-2(4)+b \\\\ -3 & =-8+b \\\\ 5 & =b \\\\ b & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-2x+5.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551228 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1069 "q-135449" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1072 #uuid "8271e0f5-ad00-5357-b8f4-39e3fffd35ad" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551298 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.39 \\\\ 0.61\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.15 & 0.45 \\\\ 0.85 & 0.55\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.39 \\\\ 0.61\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.333 \\\\ 0.667\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551133 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1069 "q-111663" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1071 "$\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}$ is equivalent to" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1072 #uuid "840068df-b5bb-5a8c-a125-569067cf40da" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551299 1074 "First, we write the expression as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{s}^{2}}{25}-\\frac{1}{81}={(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we factor the expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{s}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{9})}^{2}=(\\frac{s}{5}+\\frac{1}{9})(\\frac{s}{5}-\\frac{1}{9}) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551082 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1069 "q-18577" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1071 "$\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1072 #uuid "84195c6c-e13d-58c7-8f62-b8364f00b637" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551300 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -4 & -1 & -3 & 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\  & ↓ & 4 & -4 & 0 & -4 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-{x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+x+1}{x+4}=-{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+1-\\frac{3}{x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551209 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1069 "q-102969" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x-6)-1\\le 0.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1072 #uuid "8481b6c8-6c2f-5c14-9d27-cfb104ac96ac" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551301 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x-6)-1 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4x-6) & \\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x-6)} & \\le {10}^{1} \\\\ 4x-6 & \\le 10 \\\\ 4x & \\le 16 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n4x-6>0\\,⇒\\,x>\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 4,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,4].$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>\\frac{3}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{3}{2},\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,4]\\cap (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )=(\\frac{3}{2},4]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},4],$ which can also be written as $\\frac{3}{2}<x\\le 4.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551073 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551131 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551180 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551229 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1069 "q-269803" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{3},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-1,\\,1]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1072 #uuid "8583a74c-8e9b-50f5-aea2-c01f0aa03b2a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551302 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551095 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1069 "q-37988" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+0x-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\boxed{}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1072 #uuid "8611bb27-3bf5-5d1d-beee-a73906924e0e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551303 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+0x-1\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x+6 \\\\  & x-1\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,6{x}^{2}+0x-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6{x}^{2}-6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6x-1 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{6x-6}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $6x+6\\,$ and $\\,6x-6.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551210 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1069 "q-144037" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1071 "A cattle rancher has three ranches, where $34%$ of the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ on ranch $C.$ If $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, and the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is a dairy cow is $37%,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1072 #uuid "87509e91-72b0-5f1f-83b2-ad41631e8459" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551304 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a selected cow is a dairy cow, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the cow is from ranch $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected cow is a dairy cow, we use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since $34%$ of all the rancher's cows are on ranch $A$, $36%$ are on ranch $B,$ and $30%$ are on ranch $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.34,\\,P(B)=0.36,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $25%$ of all cows on ranch $A$ and $20%$ of all cows on ranch $B$ are dairy cows, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.25,\\,P(D|B)=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected cow from any of the three ranches is  a dairy cow is $37%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.37.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.37 & =(0.25)(0.34)+(0.2)(0.36)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.37 & =0.085+0.072+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.213 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.71\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected cow from ranch $C$ is a dairy cow is $71%.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551221 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1069 "q-139572" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1071 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1072 #uuid "8779db6b-a3a9-5c0c-95e4-31a7bc1a4418" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551305 1074 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551077 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1069 "q-140999" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1072 #uuid "896a96f2-03d3-58ec-b64e-91765762ee86" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551306 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]-0[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551097 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551307 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551140 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551307 1069 "q-343819" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [1