{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551129 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1069 "q-18548" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1071 "$\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1072 #uuid "8ccbfa58-d595-551b-aac0-8c85b253cfbe" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551308 1074 "We can calculate $\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}$ using synthetic division.\n\nCarrying out the synthetic division process, we arrive at the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{4} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -3 & 4 & 12 & 5 & 16 & 7 \\\\  & ↓ & -12 & 0 & -15 & -3 \\\\  & 4 & 0 & 5 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{4}+12{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+16x+7}{x+3}=4{x}^{3}+5x+1+\\frac{4}{x+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551118 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551157 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551223 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1069 "q-278762" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\ge \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)-3.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x\\,<$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1072 #uuid "92424f72-7efe-5e04-9bfc-7a9f038c16a4" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551309 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)-3} \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & \\ge {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4-2x)}\\cdot {e}^{-3} \\\\ (x+1) & \\ge (4-2x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{e}^{3}} \\\\ {e}^{3}(x+1) & \\ge 4-2x \\\\ x{e}^{3}+{e}^{3} & \\ge 4-2x \\\\ {e}^{3}-4 & \\ge -2x-x{e}^{3} \\\\ {e}^{3}-4 & \\ge -x(2+{e}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{3}-4}{2+{e}^{3}} & \\ge -x \\\\ x & \\ge \\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-1 \\\\ 4-2x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge \\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}},$ which can be expressed as $[\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>-1$ and $x<2,$ which can be expressed as $(-1,\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,2)$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}},\\infty )\\cap (-1,\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,2)=[\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}},2)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}},2),$ which can also be written as \n\n$$\n\\frac{4-{e}^{3}}{2+{e}^{3}}\\le x<2.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551178 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1069 "q-139645" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $9$ | $6$ | $3$ | $-2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1072 #uuid "95655e19-4263-5e26-a218-4871ce1b1f2c" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551310 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=4-2x.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551071 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551154 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551177 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551230 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1069 "q-269802" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1071 "Consider the functions $y(x)=Ax$ and $f(y)=By,$ where $x\\in {R}^{3},y\\in {R}^{2},$ and the matrices $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1,\\,3]^{T}?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))|}_{{x}_{0}}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1072 #uuid "9684ce08-aa53-5b58-9612-0d1c154cab40" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551311 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $B$ and outputs a vector $f(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}=B.\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(y(x)) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =BA \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 6 \\\\ -7 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551141 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1069 "q-119317" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\frac{a-b\\sqrt{1}4}{6},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1072 #uuid "97834400-1835-5bc3-9180-5bacac383a8e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551312 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9-2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-1\\sqrt{14}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=1,$ and $a+b=3.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551072 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551078 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551313 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551127 13194139535975 1