mputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551313 1072 #uuid "97e18a22-6fdc-5ccf-b5b0-b0a200f3d5b5" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551313 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =3$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =3,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{3t} \\\\ y={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.11$ | $0$ | $2.24$ |\n| $y$ | $0.22$ | $1$ | $4.48$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/ee/eed535cfbba657a10aa37ffd90c4de36ae85e5acfa6e44b1790ec3372da10c3c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/4e/4eaab4510bb15a7207aedaf37bff252132f62550d5dc62373aea0b506b7b0611.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551192 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1069 "q-72106" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1071 "Joseph is measuring the weight gain of his pet puppy by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since he started his observations:\n\n- $W(t)$ - The puppy's weight, in kilograms\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the puppy's weight is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $W(t)$ and $r(t)?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1072 #uuid "99da720f-6132-59c4-9760-cafcb4cb789a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551314 1074 "The function $W(t)$ gives the weight of the puppy after $t$ days. Then,\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}W(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }W}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${W}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the weight of the puppy increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $W(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${W}^{′}(t)=r(t).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551187 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1069 "q-357516" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1071 "Convert $\\frac{253}{6}$ into a repeating decimal using long division." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1072 #uuid "9c3882c6-2da8-5fcb-8c4a-eadb749133f7" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551315 1074 "To write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n253÷6\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, continuing until a repeating pattern of digits appears after the decimal point:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<br/><br/><table align=\"left\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\"></td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\,\\,4\\,2\\,.\\,1\\,6\\,6\\,…$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"20\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"left\" colspan=\"7\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{2\\,\\phantom{|}5\\,\\phantom{|}3\\,.\\,0\\,\\phantom{|}0\\,\\phantom{|}0}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$</td><td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-$</td><td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,⋮$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe could continue the division, but the same digit keeps repeating. Thus, the decimal continues with repeating $6$'s:\n\n$$\n42.166…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{253}{6}=42.166…=42.1\\overset{―}{6}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551081 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1069 "q-170688" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1071 "The earliest that a football fan arrives at a match is $15$ minutes before the beginning of the match, and the latest that they arrive is $5$ minutes late. If the arrival times are uniformly distributed, what is the mean arrival time?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1072 #uuid "9d49aabd-7000-5cc9-aab2-77d9e81c75f5" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551316 1074 "Let $X$ represent the arrival time of a randomly chosen fan relative to the beginning of the match. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[-15,5],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[-15,5],$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-15+5}{2}=-5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean arrival time is $5$ minutes before the beginning of the match." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551165 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551216 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1069 "q-326383" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{5}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{12}{13},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\,\\,+\\,$ {{field-2}}$\\sqrt{7}$ | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $52$ |  |" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1072 #uuid "9ecd9133-5002-52de-bbff-55ef2618e8b0" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551317 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{16}-\\frac{9}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{16}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{4})-(\\frac{12}{13})(-\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{52}+\\frac{12\\sqrt{7}}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12\\sqrt{7}}{52}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551113 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1069 "q-37964" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{2}+0x-9} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{2}+2x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\boxed{}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1072 #uuid "a28bf299-6ff2-586b-9620-39977848687b" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551318 1074 "Notice that the dividend is missing the $x$-term. So, we write it using a zero coefficient, as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+0x-9\n$$\n\nUsing the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-2 \\\\  & x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{2}+0x-9} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{2}+2x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-9 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,2x-4}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are\n\n$\\,$ $x-2\\,$ and $\\,-2x-4.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551182 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1069 "q-143572" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1071 "In a hardware store, there are three boxes containing light bulbs. Box $A$ contains $15$ bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. Box $B$ contains $10$ bulbs, of which $1$ is defective. And box $C$ contains $10$ light bulbs, of which $2$ are defective. A box is selected at random, and a bulb is randomly selected from the box. Find the probability that the bulb is defective." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1072 #uuid "a3aefa5f-7730-50af-bdda-78228d7a1e41" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551319 1074 "Let $D$ be the event of selecting a defective bulb, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the bulb is from box $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the bulb is from box $B$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the bulb is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space. \n\nTo calculate the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes, we can use the law of total probability: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since a box is selected at random, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(B)=P(C)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Since there are $2$ defective bulbs out of $15$  in box $A,$ $1$ defective bulb out of $10$ in box $B,$ and $2$  defective bulbs out of $10$ bulbs in box $C,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=\\frac{2}{15},\\,P(D|B)=\\frac{1}{10},\\,P(D|C)=\\frac{2}{10}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability of selecting a defective bulb at random from one of the three boxes is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{15})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{1}{10})(\\frac{1}{3})+(\\frac{2}{10})(\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{45}+\\frac{1}{30}+\\frac{2}{30} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{90}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551204 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1069 "q-141359" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, and\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\frac{\\sqrt{a}-\\sqrt{b}}{4},\n$$\n\ncalculate the value of $a+b.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1072 #uuid "a7374391-644b-5fe8-92d1-774c6f2685b8" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551320 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=6,b=2,$ and $a+b=8.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551225 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1069 "q-135453" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nConsider a Markov chain with the stochastic matrix $P$ and the initial state vector ${x}_{0}$ given above. Calculate the state vector ${x}_{2}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1072 #uuid "aad00a02-7311-5843-ab16-bebead54d45c" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551321 1074 "To find the state vector ${x}_{2},$ we apply two steps of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0},\\,{x}_{2}=P{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nAfter the first step, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.21 \\\\ 0.79\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, after the second step, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =P{x}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.35 & 0.15 \\\\ 0.65 & 0.85\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.21 \\\\ 0.79\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.192 \\\\ 0.808\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551121 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1069 "q-96924" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1071 "The minimum time it takes a pizzeria to deliver an order is $15$ minutes, and the maximum time is $27$ minutes. If the delivery time is uniformly distributed, what is the variance in the delivery time?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1072 #uuid "ac72fdfc-0b2a-5fb9-aa6a-78fe0b949c39" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551322 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time it takes to deliver a randomly selected order. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U[15,27],$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U[a,b],$ then $X$ has the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(b-a)^{2}}{12}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U[15,27],$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{(27-15)^{2}}{12}=\\frac{144}{12}=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the delivery time is $12\\,{\\text{min}}^{2}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551137 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1069 "q-91173" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1071 "$$\n|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$\n\nConsider the above $4\\times 4$ determinant. Which of the following $3\\times 3$ determinants has the same value?\n\n*Hint: Use elementary row operations to make the entries above ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1072 #uuid "adab6899-9f13-599d-9b9d-313f4a53055c" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551323 1074 "Let's make entries above ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero using elementary row operations.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{4} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th column, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & 14 \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 9 & 3 & 10\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551088 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1069 "q-266852" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1071 "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=${{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1072 #uuid "aec99370-575e-52b4-9e36-bb43c957f2bf" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551324 1074 "The angles of ${0}^{∘},$ ${45}^{∘},$ and ${90}^{∘}$ are special, and their trigonometric ratios have values\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘}=1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}=0,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡{0}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{90}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1+0-1 & = \\\\ 0. & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551201 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1069 "q-135463" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1071 "A certain store sells three types of instant soups: chicken, meat, and vegetable. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between the three types of instant soups each month:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\">From:</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Chicken</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Meat</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vegetable</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">To:</td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Chicken</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Meat</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"60\"><b>Vegetable</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nFor example, $30%$ of chicken soup customers switch to vegetable each month, and $20%$ of vegetable customers switch to meat.\n\nCurrently, $400$ customers buy chicken soup, $400$ customers buy meat soup, and $200$ customers buy vegetable soup. How many customers will purchase vegetable instant soup in one month? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1072 #uuid "af97554b-6ce7-555f-8ebb-05f560ef6317" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551325 1074 "From the table, we can build the corresponding stochastic matrix: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector describing the number of customers for each type of instant soup is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 400 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDividing the vector above by the total number of customers $(1\\,000),$ we get the stochastic vector of the initial state:\n\n$$\n{x}_{0}=\\frac{1}{1\\,000}[\\begin{aligned}400 \\\\ 400 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the state vector ${x}_{1}$ after one month, we apply one step of the Markov chain:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=P{x}_{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =P{x}_{0} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.4 & 0.2 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0.4 \\\\ 0.4 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.3 \\\\ 0.32 \\\\ 0.38\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, after one month, $38%$ of customers will buy vegetable soup. Computing $38%$ of $1\\,000,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n1\\,000\\cdot 0.38=380.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $380$ customers will buy vegetable instant soup in one month." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551219 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1069 "q-46426" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $3$ and $5$, respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $P+Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nII. $P\\cdot Q$ is always a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{P}{Q}$ is always a polynomial" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1072 #uuid "b45d9b53-e4ec-561f-955c-4e4b1f88b215" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551326 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P={x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{5}.$ Then $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551196 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1069 "q-95105" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices given above.\nFind an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables that reduces the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax$ to the canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1072 #uuid "b761dc5c-3f49-5a27-a4d6-ac454f2a31c5" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551327 1074 "Every quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nFrom the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{3}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{3}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{3}}{‖{v}_{3}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551085 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551170 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1069 "q-326377" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{8}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{15}{17},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n| $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}$\\sqrt{3}\\,-\\,$ {{field-2}} | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $34$ |  |" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1072 #uuid "bb1f9724-9227-5181-ae8d-25a299447d4f" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551328 1074 "First, we recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡v+{cos}^{2}⁡v=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡v=1-{cos}^{2}⁡v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡v & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{8}{17})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+(\\frac{15}{17})(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}}{34}-\\frac{15}{34} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{3}-15}{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551195 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1069 "q-171875" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1071 "![](images/d2/d29179efa4eaaf77d34a7c044e82c8a6bf053763adf829b83207b652d39fa531.png)\n\nIn the figure above, $m∠B=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1072 #uuid "bc6c1cea-fda3-5da9-9a26-2a572a4b6650" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551329 1074 "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is ${180}^{∘}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{74}^{∘}+(3x+{2}^{∘})+(4x-{1}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {75}^{∘}+7x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7x & ={105}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nm∠B=3x+{2}^{∘}=3({15}^{∘})+{2}^{∘}={47}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551069 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551104 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551114 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551132 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1069 "q-331792" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1071 "Consider a function $w(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{w(t)\\}=W(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1072 #uuid "bf25d1c4-52d6-5c8a-89b9-2f0b3253eb28" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551330 1074 "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-6t}w(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-6t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $W(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+6)t}\\,w(t)\\,\\text{d}t=W(s+6),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+6>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-6.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551058 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551076 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551123 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551162 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551183 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551203 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551208 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551211 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1069 "q-333304" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{{field-1}}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}{{field-2}},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the \n{{field-3}} \nSince ${e}^{4t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-6}} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/36/36ae671944545eaae9027ef0d4a876e12ceef17d01be0ad881d32dfdcb8ad963.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1072 #uuid "bff2c616-f045-5186-8916-8acfc055fce7" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551331 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =4$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x\\text{-axis}.$ Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move toward $\\text{infinity}$ in the phase plane as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/27/27e921a76383c4f8a4fc1e81a1febcfad53f330d0e902f4e07578238da33422f.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{4t} \\\\ y={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.07$ | $0$ | $1.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.14$ | $1$ | $4.95$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/fd/fd431b8daf3ad9c1b25225af9d64b1e4876e4e1a90e1dfd010de8ed134ae323c.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1fd20f7333800323c44f41d7906958a0928d5f30279e36b1f50d0ca1f66fcd46.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551159 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1069 "q-139574" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1071 "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $15$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the design matrix $X$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1072 #uuid "c02b9ef4-844a-58b2-be01-9b59908adb2c" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551332 1074 "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-4$** | **$-2$** | **$0$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $15$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 11 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551145 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1069 "q-167106" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(10,3)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1072 #uuid "c6760dcc-4403-5b3c-b44a-9e304dd2dc4f" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551333 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-2,$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-10) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551189 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1069 "q-52490" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1071 "$\\sqrt[3]{-{x}^{3}}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1072 #uuid "c6c9bca9-ea70-5989-b840-49d437c3ef76" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551334 1074 "We write the term under the radical as a perfect cube, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{-{x}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{-x\\cdot x\\cdot x} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-x)\\cdot (-x)\\cdot (-x)} & = \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{(-x)^{3}} & = \\\\ -x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551138 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1069 "q-51691" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1071 "Given that $P(x)$ is a polynomial of degree $5,$ which of the following statements are always true?\n\nI. $P(x)-4{x}^{6}$ is a polynomial\n\nII. $4{x}^{6}\\cdot P(x)$ is a polynomial\n\nIII. $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}$ is a polynomial" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1072 #uuid "ceee5cde-40e3-565c-b032-fa1a9c5be489" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551335 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Polynomials are closed under subtraction.\n\n- Statement II is true. Polynomials are closed under multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P(x)={x}^{5}+1.$ Then $\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{P(x)}=\\frac{4{x}^{6}}{{x}^{5}+1},$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551205 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1069 "q-134560" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1071 "$7\\sqrt[3]{21}-3\\sqrt[3]{21}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1072 #uuid "d56a0cf9-3169-596f-ae20-a8cf3a250f28" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551336 1074 "We can combine the two $\\sqrt[3]{21}$ terms together, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\sqrt[3]{21}-3\\sqrt[3]{21} & = \\\\ (7-3)\\sqrt[3]{21} & = \\\\ 4\\sqrt[3]{21} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551117 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1069 "q-41401" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1071 "The height (in feet) and the average number of points scored per game (APG) of four players on a basketball team are shown in the following table. \n\n| Height $(x)$ | **$6$** | **$6.5$** | **$6.5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| APG $(y)$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}?$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1072 #uuid "d6dbf98c-84c4-5c67-a15a-4f405cbbd0b5" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551337 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 6.5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}84.75 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}33 \\\\ 217\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-24.25 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-24.25+5x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft},$  we substitute $x=6.45$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-24.25+5(6.45) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model,  the average number of points scored per game by a player whose height is $6.45\\,\\text{ft}$ will be $8$  points per game." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551161 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1069 "q-254908" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1071 "Suppose $a=[2,\\,-7,\\,1]^{T}$ and $b=[-3,\\,-1,\\,5]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1072 #uuid "d6f36bc2-1e9d-59b6-b406-9f6515dd271d" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551338 1074 "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|2-(-3)|,|-7-(-1)|,|1-5|) \\\\  & =max(5,6,4) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551092 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1069 "q-7002" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1071 "For the function $f(x)$ defined by $f(x)=2x-3,$ find ${f}^{-1}(x).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1072 #uuid "d76f6fd1-8c28-5a6f-b6d6-f8935ad11046" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551339 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=2x-3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=2y-3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2y-3 \\\\ x+3 & =2y \\\\ \\frac{x+3}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+3}{2}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551175 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1069 "q-139653" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-25$ | $5$ | $20$ | $35$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}$ that best fits the data above can be found using polynomial regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1072 #uuid "d9bbc936-cdf8-5bb2-be25-3df4f1bc3232" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551340 1074 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$1$** | **$3$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-5$ | $-25$ | $5$ | $20$ | $35$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & (-3)^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1 & (-1)^{2} \\\\ 1 & 1 & {1}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 3 & {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & 5 & {5}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -25 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551218 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1069 "q-5991" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1071 "$1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1072 #uuid "da211e88-d789-5f6a-b87d-2366e18b07f4" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551341 1074 "The angle ${30}^{∘}$ is special, and its sine has the value\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} & = \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551207 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1069 "q-122353" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1071 "![](images/77/77242f149dc99e0641e6e095d02aeeb1fffdf764ee664cfcc02e659f9d0e03b0.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ rounded to one decimal place." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1072 #uuid "db59e458-8e99-5a95-814f-8adc8a6a9836" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551342 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =7 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(0.927\\,18…)} \\\\  & =7.549\\,74… \\\\  & ≈7.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551066 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551148 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1069 "q-215134" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1071 "Written in **ascending order**, the solutions to $|9-y|=4$ are $y=${{field-1}} and $y=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1072 #uuid "dbb8c729-ab7f-5cd4-a54a-d589b9fc5814" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551343 1074 "Either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nSo, we set up the two corresponding equations and solve them independently:\n\n- If $|9-y|=9-y,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|9-y| & =4 \\\\ 9-y & =4 \\\\ -y & =-5 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|9-y|=-(9-y),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|9-y| & =4 \\\\ -(9-y) & =4 \\\\ -9+y & =4 \\\\ y & =13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $y=5$ and $y=13.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551103 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1069 "q-119320" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=-\\frac{2}{7},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1072 #uuid "dbced5cb-cb8f-554f-bc5c-eb4739e6f20e" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551344 1074 "First, recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡u=1-{sin}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{5}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the sum formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u+v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})+(\\frac{2}{3})(-\\frac{2}{7}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{21}-\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551198 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1069 "q-130775" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1071 "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1072 #uuid "dd52658d-d0dc-5c1e-b084-552fde414f6a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551345 1074 "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551153 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1069 "q-140988" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1072 #uuid "e2384f15-0b1d-5ffb-9cdd-5f07e0af2003" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551346 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,2,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n5{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551100 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551116 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551126 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551186 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1069 "q-342492" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1072 #uuid "e3e7079a-616c-59e7-8130-14cd0fb2782d" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551347 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/66/66b91f732d36469ded18df5404b3f12dddad7474168efd47ddf038835b67b831.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(-2x+1)=-2(-3)+1=7\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}({x}^{2}+1)=(3)^{2}+1=10.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/88/888053858d845bd47a56b953a5eb9a6f7f6b4ddffc0ecc70fa280e1a7d543f24.png)" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551065 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551124 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551144 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551174 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1069 "q-343391" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=3{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)={x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1072 #uuid "ed658204-766e-56cd-a12e-1cf2b14b40f7" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551348 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=\\pi $ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ \nTherefore, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}({\\pi }^{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nt))\\text{d}t \\\\ [{t}^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[{\\pi }^{2}t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nt)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ {x}^{3} & ∼{\\pi }^{2}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{12(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551098 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1069 "q-269823" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1071 "For $x,y,z\\in {R}^{2},$ consider the following functions: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y(x)=Ax\\,\\text{where}\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & z(y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & f(z)=Cz\\,\\text{where}\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))$ evaluated at ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1072 #uuid "ed9f515f-42d2-575d-bafa-bcd19e12a760" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551349 1074 "Using the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x)))=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each of the factors in turn.\n\n- The function $f$ acts on a vector $z\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $C$ and outputs a vector $f(z)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}=C.\n$$\n\n- The function $z$ acts on a vector $y\\in {R}^{2}$ and outputs a vector $z(y)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{1}}{∂{y}_{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{1}} & \\frac{∂{z}_{2}}{∂{y}_{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}_{1} & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEvaluating at ${y}_{0}=A{x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}=[\\begin{aligned}2(-1) & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- The function $y$ acts on a vector $x\\in {R}^{2}$ by multiplying it by $A$ and outputs a vector $y(x)\\in {R}^{2}.$ Thus,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}f(z(y(x))){|}_{{x}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}z}{|}_{{z}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}y}{|}_{{y}_{0}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551086 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1069 "q-139577" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1071 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector. Which of the following is the observation vector $y$ in this context?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1072 #uuid "edc742b5-3b4b-536d-82ef-b613c6a8e953" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551350 1074 "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $5$ | $10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551109 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1069 "q-94970" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1071 "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=7{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1072 #uuid "f4e82a18-a51f-5302-8e3f-b37a2f43e1e2" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551351 1074 "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +36) & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -9) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =9,\\,7,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n9{y}_{1}^{2}+7{y}_{2}^{2}+4{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551080 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551152 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1069 "q-343906" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1072 #uuid "f846bdbb-b4eb-53cc-9341-26d00f9d6239" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551352 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=9 \\\\ 2\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{9}{2\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{9x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551108 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1069 "q-144400" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡v=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡v=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡u=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, find the value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v).$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1072 #uuid "f8787df3-5e2e-5b10-9759-f8010831da06" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551353 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡v$, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡v$, and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡u.$ To find the missing value of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡u$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡u+{cos}^{2}⁡u=1\\,⟹\\,{sin}^{2}⁡u=1-{cos}^{2}⁡u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{4}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡u=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-(-\\frac{2}{3})(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{6}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-\\sqrt{15}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551079 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551084 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1069 "q-167098" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1071 "Write the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines that pass through the point $B.$\n\nThe **equation** of the vertical line is \n{{field-1}}\n\nThe **equation** of the horizontal line is \n{{field-2}}" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1072 #uuid "fc8a9725-c277-5a2e-a217-f21f271d6767" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551354 1074 "The coordinates of $B$ are $(1,3).$\n\n* Since the vertical line should pass through $(1,3)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\nx=1.\n$$\n\n* Since the horizontal line should pass through $(1,3)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $3.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\ny=3.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551150 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1069 "q-37755" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1071 "Consider the following polynomial division:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6{x}^{2}-13x-5 \\\\  & -x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}-21x+10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}-21x+10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{13{x}^{2}-26x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x+10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the above, $\\frac{-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}-21x+10}{-x+2}=$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1072 #uuid "fd8cef0f-9754-53fc-971e-657a61c10786" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551355 1074 "$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,6{x}^{2}-13x-5 \\\\  & -x+2\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}-21x+10\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}-21x+10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{13{x}^{2}-26x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,5x+10 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{5x-10}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given polynomial division, we deduce that dividing the polynomial $-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}-21x+10$ by the linear binomial $-x+2$ gives\n\n- the quotient $6{x}^{2}-13x-5,$ and\n\n- the remainder $20.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}-21x+10}{-x+2}=6{x}^{2}-13x-5+\\frac{20}{-x+2}.\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551061 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1069 "q-105592" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1071 "From top to bottom, what are the missing expressions in the following polynomial division problem?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\boxed{} \\\\  & 2x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+2x+14\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{2}+2x+14 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\boxed{}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8x+14 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{8x+12}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1072 #uuid "fe67be82-4bb6-56a3-b9ff-5fcade759364" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551356 1074 "Using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-2x+4 \\\\  & 2x+3\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+2x+14\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,4{x}^{2}+2x+14 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,4{x}^{2}-6x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,8x+14 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{8x+12}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the missing expressions are \n\n$\\,$ $2{x}^{2}-2x+4\\,$ and $\\,-4{x}^{2}-6x.$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551091 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1069 "q-113885" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1072 #uuid "fea7dc0c-332b-5e65-b34c-aa74602e656a" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551357 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+1)^{2},$ the lower bound is $a=2,$ and the upper bound is $b=4.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{4-2}{n}=\\frac{2}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(2+\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(2+\\frac{2i}{n}+1)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(3+\\frac{2i}{n})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551358 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551390 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551358 1062 #uuid "015fd7cc-296c-527b-8d93-783a3144a040" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551358 1063 #edb/ref 17592186436225 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551359 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551415 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551359 1062 #uuid "01b4628b-63b7-5c96-87a8-416f13d0e71b" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551359 1063 #edb/ref 17592186354621 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551360 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551428 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551360 1062 #uuid "01c39290-cd5a-5aa1-8a59-9cbfefffe951" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551360 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551311 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551361 1058 #edb/ref 17592186551443 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551361 1062 #uuid "02cb4930-9c19-5e79-99d9-884aeb31e740" 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551361 1063 #edb/ref 17592186551242 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186551362 1058 #edb/ref